  "events": [
    {
      "text": "Green wings after dusk. Inspired by the Missouri Department of Conservation luna moth field guide: https://mdc.mo.gov/discover-nature/field-guide/luna-moth . I am painting an original moth study against dusky violet. Goal: filled pale wing masses, varied edges and selective lights. Broad shapes first; eyespots and veins later.",
      "kind": "notice",
      "camera": {
        "zoom": 1,
        "x": 0.5,
        "y": 0.5
      },
      "duration": 11440,
      "afterStroke": 0
    },
    {
      "kind": "look",
      "camera": {
        "zoom": 2,
        "x": 0.5,
        "y": 0.5
      },
      "duration": 1200,
      "afterStroke": 3
    },
    {
      "text": "The violet washes have soft granular edges, but two pale seams divide the field. I will bridge those first and lay the first broad left wing as a filled mass, keeping its outer tip tapered.",
      "kind": "notice",
      "camera": {
        "zoom": 2,
        "x": 0.5,
        "y": 0.5
      },
      "duration": 9100,
      "afterStroke": 3
    },
    {
      "kind": "look",
      "camera": {
        "zoom": 1,
        "x": 0.5,
        "y": 0.5
      },
      "duration": 1200,
      "afterStroke": 6
    },
    {
      "kind": "look",
      "camera": {
        "zoom": 2,
        "x": 0.5,
        "y": 0.5
      },
      "duration": 1200,
      "afterStroke": 6
    },
    {
      "text": "The bridging washes removed the bright seams. The left forewing is a solid tapered mass, with regular oil ridges still visible. I will place a slightly higher right forewing and two rounder hindwings, leaving room for long tails.",
      "kind": "notice",
